// Relativization / partition-safety, `count` aggregation.
//
// A Read is permitted iff THIS principal has at least two of its own Logins in
// the last hour. The event schema pins `callerPrincipal` universally, so the
// count ranges over this principal's own events only — another principal's
// logins must not count toward the threshold.
//
// Two differential pairs, each `_with_foreign` vs `_without_foreign`:
//
//   DENY pair — alice below threshold, foreign logins must not lift her over:
//     trace_1: alice Login @1, BOB Login @2, BOB Login @3, alice Read @4.
//     trace_2: alice Login @1,                             alice Read @4.
//   alice has ONE own Login in both, count 1 (< 2) → DENY in both. Under
//   (incorrect) global semantics trace_1 would count bob's two logins
//   (total 3 >= 2) and PERMIT, diverging from trace_2.
//
//   PERMIT pair — alice at threshold on her OWN events, foreign login is inert:
//     trace_3: alice Login @1, BOB Login @2, alice Login @3, alice Read @4.
//     trace_4: alice Login @1,               alice Login @3, alice Read @4.
//   alice has TWO own Logins in both, count 2 (>= 2) → PERMIT in both; bob's
//   interleaved login neither adds nor removes.
//
// The rewrite confines the count to alice's own events, so each pair agrees.
//
// All Login events carry `input.user: _` (unconstrained) — the count body does
// not correlate on user at all, so the pinned `callerPrincipal` is the ONLY
// thing confining the count to alice; without it bob's logins would leak in.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    exists (n: Long). (
        (count for (t: Timepoint). where (
            formerly within 1h (Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: _ } && tp(t))
        )) == n && n >= 2
    )
};
